{"sha":"a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699","node_id":"C_kwDOTzCqitoAKGE1NzM4M2ZkMzRmYWVjM2E2ZmY4OTFiNWM2M2YwYTlmNTM1ZWQ2OTk","commit":{"author":{"name":"Jérôme Piquot","email":"jpiquot@itaneo.com","date":"2026-10-30T17:56:20Z"},"committer":{"name":"Jérôme Piquot","email":"jpiquot@itaneo.com","date":"2026-10-30T17:56:20Z"},"message":"docs: apply the story 5.1 staging evidence policy corrections","tree":{"sha":"3a41b15d397ef97d73838af28bf5da621da34c0c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/git/trees/3a41b15d397ef97d73838af28bf5da621da34c0c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/git/commits/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699","comment_count":0,"verification":{"verified":false,"reason":"unsigned","signature":null,"payload":null,"verified_at":null}},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/commits/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699","html_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/commit/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/commits/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/comments","author":{"login":"jpiquot","id":6775094,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY3NzUwOTQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6775094?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot","html_url":"https://github.com/jpiquot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"committer":{"login":"jpiquot","id":6775094,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY3NzUwOTQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6775094?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot","html_url":"https://github.com/jpiquot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jpiquot/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"667e5a02a523cf8764ca54d6aa1b4b75db5dca64","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/commits/667e5a02a523cf8764ca54d6aa1b4b75db5dca64","html_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/commit/667e5a02a523cf8764ca54d6aa1b4b75db5dca64"}],"stats":{"total":319,"additions":84,"deletions":235},"files":[{"sha":"01ce961e0b60158b7f86fa7ecaad44ec931c2693","filename":"_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/deferred-work.md","status":"modified","additions":0,"deletions":6,"changes":6,"blob_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/blob/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fdeferred-work.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/raw/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fdeferred-work.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/contents/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fdeferred-work.md?ref=a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699","patch":"@@ -1611,9 +1611,3 @@\n   summary: Decide vulnerability coverage for images that run in production but are not in the release inventory, such as injected sidecars and shared infrastructure (unverified, medium if true).\n   target_story: 5.5\n   evidence: Policy v1 scans \"every included\" release image and reconciles against the release inventory. Settle by confirming whether production pods for a release run images outside the release record (e.g. Dapr sidecar injection from the cluster installation) and whether Epic 6's shared-infrastructure procedure scans them.\n-\n-## Deferred from: fifth-pass corrections build review of spec-5-1-decide-the-staging-evidence-policies (2026-10-30)\n-\n-- source_spec: `_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/spec-5-1-decide-the-staging-evidence-policies.md`\n-  summary: Settle whether a later candidate's environment-layer attempt removes objects declared only by an additive unadopted candidate that needed no reset (medium if true, unverified).\n-  evidence: Build review EC4. The architecture's Staging reset row and policy decision 5 skip resets for proven baseline-readable additive writes, and only a reset candidate's declared-only environment-layer objects are removed by the later candidate's environment-layer attempt. If an additive candidate's declared-only objects remain, a later candidate could pass staging while relying on objects production lacks. Settle in Story 5.10, or in the architecture, by confirming whether environment-layer application prunes objects the applied release does not declare."},{"sha":"0899aa28a06717e6970a894f02eff2457752cfbc","filename":"_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/epic-5-context.md","status":"modified","additions":32,"deletions":54,"changes":86,"blob_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/blob/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fepic-5-context.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/raw/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fepic-5-context.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/contents/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fepic-5-context.md?ref=a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699","patch":"@@ -36,73 +36,51 @@ Promote the same immutable Helm package and images qualified in staging. The rel\n \n One serialized environment attempt holds the lock and monotonic epoch across preparation, deployment and evidence collection. Cut a staging recovery point before candidate writes. Executors keep module-supplied E2E and smoke code sandboxed away from deployment credentials. Provenance, isolation and the staging gate apply equally to both release modes; Administrator approval does not waive them.\n \n-Without a production baseline or retained application data, qualify fresh installation without inventing a rollback target. With a baseline and eligible compatibility evidence, start from that baseline, upgrade to the exact candidate and rehearse its prepared rollback set with HotReload off. Exercise a candidate-new idempotent command and verify that baseline code reads candidate state and events. A failed rollback rehearsal is breaking compatibility evidence. Staging catalog, key and secret retirements follow AD-15's expand-only rule relative to production's working baseline.\n+Without a production baseline or retained application data, qualify fresh installation without inventing a rollback target. With a baseline and eligible compatibility evidence, start from that baseline, upgrade to the exact candidate and rehearse its prepared rollback set with HotReload off. Exercise a candidate-new idempotent command and verify that baseline code reads candidate state and events. A failed rollback rehearsal is breaking compatibility evidence. Catalog, key and secret retirements remain expand-only relative to the working baseline.\n \n-A retained-data approved attempt lacking safe compatibility needs a named, rehearsed data recovery with retained artifacts, a recovery point, authority, verification and lost-window treatment; it prepares no automatic rollback set. An unadopted candidate's unreadable or unproven-readability writes require a verified in-place staging restore as a separate attempt before a later candidate starts or a same-candidate rerun. Retain recovery points under the policy's retention rule, regenerate the staging realm and remove candidate-only environment objects. Additive candidates need no reset.\n+A retained-data approved attempt lacking safe compatibility needs a named, rehearsed data recovery with retained artifacts, a recovery point, authority, verification and lost-window treatment; it prepares no automatic rollback set. An unadopted candidate's incompatible writes require an in-place staging restore as a separate attempt before a later candidate starts. Keep referenced recovery points, regenerate the staging realm and remove candidate-only environment objects. Additive candidates need no reset.\n \n ## Cross-Story Dependencies\n \n The approved [staging evidence policy v1](../../docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md)\n (`hexalith.platform.staging-evidence-policy.v1`, Story 5.1) is the canonical\n decision record for Stories 5.3, 5.6–5.11 and the related lifecycle review in\n-Story 5.4. It fixes:\n-\n-- **Evidence age.** Evidence has a 24-hour (86,400-second) exclusive deadline\n-  from attempt start, checked at promotion admission.\n-- **Triggers.** A retained candidate passing intake opens a pending decision and\n-  queues an attempt automatically. Checked-input and baseline changes queue\n-  attempts under that decision. Checked inputs include the preparation branch and\n-  the branch-determining content of the Administrator approval selecting it.\n-  Re-intake of a release with a pending decision queues nothing by itself.\n-  Intake of the release production's latest-working pointer currently names opens\n-  no decision and queues no attempt. An attempt whose decision is no longer\n-  pending when it reaches the staging lock is cancelled with its reason; it never\n-  starts and supersedes nothing. The Maintainer may request a fresh attempt for a\n-  pending decision after expiry or a failed, cancelled, incomplete, interrupted or\n-  superseded E2E terminal outcome. There is no periodic retry loop.\n-- **Reruns.** Only the latest issued serialized E2E attempt can qualify, with a\n-  full passing rerun. History is preserved, evidence is never reused across\n-  decisions, and reset or named-recovery evidence never replaces an E2E attempt.\n-  A decision closes as completed when production's latest-working pointer names\n-  its release, before that baseline change queues triggers, or as superseded.\n-  Superseded decisions refuse admission and receive no automatic triggers.\n-- **Flow review.** The owning module and Platform approve flow removal/remap\n-  against each applicable latest-accepted and production-baseline predecessor.\n-  Tenants reviews every lifecycle flow before enrollment and before any changed\n-  declaration joins the gate, and also approves lifecycle-flow removal/remap.\n-- **Reset lifetime.** The reset lifetime is 60 minutes (3,600 seconds) from\n-  recovery entry. The off-site monitor notifies Administrator, the recovery\n-  deputy and the Platform Maintainer through the architecture's accepted\n-  private-issue path. It does not abort restoration or unblock later candidates\n-  before verification. Executor loss interrupts the reset and leaves it\n-  non-terminal, so the lifetime notification still applies, until re-entry\n-  records it superseded. An observation gap, a failed or incomplete restore or\n-  failed verification leaves the reset unverified. Only the initial reset is\n-  issued automatically. After executor loss or an unverified reset,\n-  Administrator or the deputy re-enters from the same recovery point in a\n-  distinct attempt. The re-entry\n-  inherits the prior deadline before it expires and records entry plus 3,600\n-  seconds at or after it.\n-- **Reset triggers.** Unreadable or unproven-readability candidate writes,\n-  including without a production baseline, require a verified reset before a\n-  same-candidate rerun or later candidate. The reset uses the earliest pre-write\n-  point since the last verified reset.\n-- **Preparation.** Missing applicable preparation proof or an unmatched state\n-  refuses admission.\n-\n-Qualification also depends on Epic 6. Story 5.6 completed closure requires\n-Story 6.16's latest-working pointer move. Story 5.10 reset qualification\n-requires Story 6.4's off-site monitor for maximum-lifetime notification, Story\n-6.3's recovery authority for deputy re-entry and Story 6.15's takeover fencing.\n-Story 5.11 admission qualification requires Story 6.14's precondition 4 check\n-under the production lock and new epoch.\n+Story 5.4. It fixes a 24-hour (86,400-second) exclusive evidence deadline from\n+attempt start at promotion admission; automatic attempts for retained intake\n+candidates or checked-input/baseline changes under a pending decision, including\n+the preparation branch and the Administrator approval selecting it, with\n+explicit Maintainer requests for pending decisions after expiry or failed,\n+cancelled, incomplete, interrupted or superseded E2E terminal outcomes and no\n+periodic retry loop; full passing reruns\n+from only the latest issued serialized E2E attempt, preserving history without\n+cross-decision reuse or replacement by reset/named-recovery evidence; and\n+owning-module and Platform approval of flow removal/remap against each applicable\n+latest-accepted and production-baseline predecessor, with Tenants review of every\n+lifecycle flow before enrollment and before any changed declaration joins the\n+gate, including Tenants approval of lifecycle-flow removal/remap. Superseded\n+decisions refuse admission and receive no automatic triggers.\n+\n+The reset lifetime is 60 minutes (3,600 seconds) from recovery entry. The off-site\n+monitor notifies Administrator, the recovery deputy and the Platform Maintainer\n+through the architecture's accepted private-issue path without aborting\n+restoration or unblocking later candidates before verification. Executor loss\n+fails the reset; an observation gap leaves it unverified. Administrator or the\n+deputy re-enters from the same point in a distinct attempt, inheriting the prior\n+deadline before it expires and recording entry plus 3,600 seconds at or after it.\n+Unreadable or unproven-readability candidate writes, including without a\n+production baseline, require a verified reset before a same-candidate rerun or\n+later candidate, using the earliest pre-write point since the last verified\n+reset. Missing applicable preparation proof or an unmatched state refuses\n+admission. Story 5.10 reset qualification requires Story 6.4's off-site monitor\n+for maximum-lifetime notification and Story 6.3's recovery authority for deputy\n+re-entry.\n \n Platform Maintainer `@jpiquot` is accountable. Builds and Platform must implement\n and qualify adoption; modules, Platform and Tenants must record their required\n flow approvals and reviews, and Administrator and EventStore must qualify resets.\n Publication does not establish enforcement, record unprovided owner approvals or\n authorize promotion.\n \n-The owned reference check suites precede declaration validation. Staging policy decisions precede evidence acceptance; the vulnerability decision precedes scan enforcement. Tenants and module owners review lifecycle flows before enrollment and before any changed declaration joins the gate. Module recovery hooks are prerequisites for staging resets and applicable named recoveries.\n+The owned reference check suites precede declaration validation. Staging policy decisions precede evidence acceptance; the vulnerability decision precedes scan enforcement. Tenants and module owners review lifecycle flows before gate enrollment. Module recovery hooks are prerequisites for staging resets and applicable named recoveries.\n \n The no-baseline branch enables the first production deployment. Baseline-dependent rollback and retained-data qualification follow establishment of the first working production baseline and must close before accepting a second production candidate. Only the Platform publication workflow may turn an attested McpCli candidate into a stable version after the complete applicable staging gate passes."},{"sha":"71de8aeca65e07164c7ad711ab0e3edb657b558b","filename":"_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/spec-5-1-decide-the-staging-evidence-policies.md","status":"modified","additions":0,"deletions":81,"changes":81,"blob_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/blob/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fspec-5-1-decide-the-staging-evidence-policies.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/raw/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fspec-5-1-decide-the-staging-evidence-policies.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/contents/_bmad-output%2Fimplementation-artifacts%2Fspec-5-1-decide-the-staging-evidence-policies.md?ref=a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699","patch":"@@ -160,51 +160,6 @@ Rejected (fourth pass):\n - E20 (moving pending v1 decisions to v2), low: no later version exists. L280-284 already refuse mismatched identifiers, and a transition rule belongs to v2's decision.\n - E21 (sprint status still at review), false: repeats B9. Review is the workflow's intermediate status, and this review updates it.\n \n-Fifth-pass code review 2026-10-30 of the Story 5.1 deliverables from baseline `2cbb8a68` to `2030542f`: the policy, the README staging-evidence paragraph (the Story 5.2 vulnerability paragraph excluded) and the epic context. Layers: Blind Hunter (16), Edge Case Hunter (21), Verification Gap (no gaps) and Acceptance Auditor (7). Line references are to `docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md` unless stated.\n-\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Re-intake of a release with a pending decision (decision 2026-10-30: adopted (a)) [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:93] — L93-94 queue an attempt whenever a retained candidate passes intake, while L118-121 and the L359 example say only that re-intake opens no new decision. Read literally, re-intake queues a newer attempt that blocks fallback to an existing fresh pass (L144-145), although a manual rerun in that state is refused (L107-109). Options: (a) re-intake of a release with a pending decision opens no decision and queues no attempt by itself; checked-input changes still queue under decision 2 (recommended); (b) re-intake queues a fresh complete attempt under the pending decision. Either way, L119 \"unless that release already has one\" should say \"a pending one\", so completed or superseded releases return only under a new decision (L138-140).\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Which Administrator approval is a checked input (decision 2026-10-30: adopted (a)) [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:95] — L95-98 make \"the Administrator approval selecting that branch\" a checked input, but the architecture's Release modes and Empty or degraded production records are per production attempt (the record \"names one attempt\" and its observed stop revision). Options: (a) bind the branch-determining content (the selected path and, for the retained-data path, the named recovery's identity, acceptance checks and maximum duration), so a new per-attempt record repeating that content is no checked-input change (recommended); (b) bind the exact record identity, so every new production approval queues a fresh staging attempt.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Evidence bindings omit the preparation branch and its selecting approval [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:42] — L95-98 and the L357 example make both checked inputs, but the L42-51 binding list never records them, so admission (L57-58) has nothing to compare; epics Story 5.6 requires the attempt record to name the preparation state. Add both to the binding list (approval content per the decision above) and to the Story 5.6 row.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Executor loss \"fails\" a reset that the architecture leaves non-terminal [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:245] — L232-234 notify a non-terminal reset \"even if the executor has died\", but L245-246, the L371 example and the epic context say executor loss fails it. Under the architecture a dead executor leaves the lock held and its record non-terminal (Attempt ownership); the Administrator or deputy re-entry takes it over and records its terminal outcome as superseded (Attempt resumption; Binding classes and records). Say executor loss interrupts the reset and leaves it non-terminal, so the lifetime notification still applies, until the re-entry records it superseded. The adopted no-replacement-job and same-point re-entry rules stay.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Recovery-point retention never ends [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:216] — \"any reset or recovery reference remains\" covers permanent history lineage (L152-155, L225-226), so a literal Story 5.10 implementation never prunes. The architecture's Recovery point and freshness row skips only points referenced by an open recovery, a pending named recovery or an unadopted candidate's reset, and the L374 example says \"pending reset reference\". Restrict the clause to open or pending references.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Handoff omits the Epic 6 executors and completed closure [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:333] — no consumer closes a decision as completed (L131-132), so the adopted release's decision stays pending; its own baseline change (L98-99) can queue an attempt for it, and a later candidate's start can supersede it. Promotion admission runs in Story 6.14's precondition 4 (L53-56), the working baseline moves in Story 6.16, and reset re-entry fencing belongs to Story 6.15. Assign completed closure to the Story 5.6 row, applied when production's latest-working pointer names the release and before that baseline change queues triggers, and record Stories 6.14, 6.15 and 6.16 as prerequisites in the Story 5.6, 5.10 and 5.11 rows and in the epic context.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] \"Superseded\" names three different things [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:143] — a taken-over attempt (L104-106), a decision closure defined only in decision 5 (L220-223, cited at L131) and, through \"determines which attempt supersedes the others\", an earlier attempt that is no longer latest. The last reading relabels an earlier pass, contradicting L154-155. Define a superseded attempt (architecture Attempt resumption) and a superseded decision (decision 5) at first use, and say issuance order selects the latest attempt without changing earlier recorded outcomes.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Remaining requirements written as plain imperatives [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:227] — L227 \"record `resetLifetimeDeadline`\", L208-209 \"treat those writes as unreadable and require the same reset\" and L166 \"treat the transition as a removal or remap\" are requirements without MUST (L8).\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Reset-expiry recipients omit the recovery deputy [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:233] — the bold MUST names Administrator and the Platform Maintainer, while the notification path (L237-238), the L370 example, the epic context and the architecture Roles row include the deputy.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Expand-only sentence contradicts itself [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:299] — \"retirements MUST remain expand-only\" reads as a contraction staying an expansion. Restate the architecture Staging gate row: staging catalog, key and secret retirements MUST follow AD-15's expand-only rule relative to production's working baseline.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Decision 4 accountability row omits Tenants [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:311] — the owner column names the Maintainer, modules and Builds, though Tenants reviews every lifecycle flow and approves lifecycle removal/remap (AC 1 requires each rule's owners).\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Removal/remap example leaves \"matching digests\" unexplained [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:366] — a removal changes the declaration digest against its predecessor; say the recorded and declared digests agree (FR-6's \"matching digests alone\").\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] \"Existing Platform publication workflow\" implies an implemented workflow [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:344] — `.github/workflows/` holds only `tuple-ci.yml`; say \"the architecture's Platform publication workflow\" to avoid an enforcement claim.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Story 5.11 row omits stable McpCli publication refusal [docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md:342] — epics Story 5.11 qualifies it and L344-345 state the rule; the existing F4 deferral keeps the evaluation-boundary question.\n-- [x] [Review][Patch] Epic-context deadline wording reads as measured at admission [_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/epic-5-context.md:47] — \"deadline from attempt start at promotion admission\" should say \"checked at promotion admission\", and the L53-54 rewrap leaves a short line.\n-\n-Carried (fifth pass): E7 (supersession during an admitted production attempt), E8 (reserved candidate's rerun after a later candidate) and E18 (no usable pre-write recovery point) keep their existing fourth-pass deferrals; no new ledger entry.\n-\n-Rejected (fifth pass):\n-\n-- B1/E21/A7 (v1 fixed revision unrecorded), low, process-only: the adopted D1 (a) wording fixes v1 at the revision completing Story 5.1, which git history identifies once sprint status records done; no adopter pins it yet, and recording a digest needs a new mechanism or a spec edit.\n-- B4 (check-body weakening outside removal/remap), low: FR-6 and frozen decision 4 scope review to declarations and mappings; a suite-digest change already forces a fresh complete attempt, and reviewing every test edit adds mechanism.\n-- B6 (no manual rerun for a fresh pass refused on mismatch), low: a mismatch without an input change implies an executor defect, the evidence expires within 24 hours and then permits a request, and widening frozen decision 2 adds a state.\n-- B8 (synthetic-tenant exclusion omitted), false: Story 5.4 AC 2 and the epic context carry the exclusion; the policy restates only review constraints, and the frozen Never excludes tenant implementation.\n-- B10 (no E2E attempt lifetime or interruption rule), false: the architecture's Timing and interruption and Attempt ownership rows give every attempt a kind and maximum lifetime (Story 6.15), and resumption performs only remaining recovery (earlier E7/A11).\n-- B11 (no Platform Maintainer role source; unreachable mention), false: CODEOWNERS assigns Administrator to `@jpiquot`, the policy names `@jpiquot`, and as Administrator that identity is a named writer the mention reaches. The deputy omission is patched separately.\n-- B14 (circular preparation selection), low: with a baseline and no Administrator approval, row 3 is the only applicable row at start, and precondition 9's change classification precedes the rehearsal; restructuring the table exceeds a direct fix.\n-- E1 (clock skew), low: repeats E1/B10.\n-- E2 (timestamp precision), low: the same executor record computes start and expiry; a canonical format is a Story 5.6 schema choice, outside the frozen Never.\n-- E3 (pending decision with no issued attempt), low: requires a lost automatic trigger; repeats the fourth-pass E7 rationale and would add a permitted state.\n-- E4 (coalescing queued attempts), low: repeats E4/B3.\n-- E6 (abandoned pending decision), low: arises only if no later candidate ever stages; a withdrawal closure is new mechanism, and v1 defines no other closure.\n-- E9 (readability proven against an earlier baseline), false: L207-209 key the reset on the current working baseline; proof against a replaced baseline leaves readability unproven, which requires the reset.\n-- E10 (unknown write occurrence), false: repeats fourth-pass E11.\n-- E11 (dropping a whole module), false: repeats R3.\n-- E12 (tenant suspend/rename flows), false: FR-6 and Story 5.4 AC 2 limit the lifecycle restriction to tenant creation and deletion.\n-- E13 (unmarked tenant from an unreviewed flow), false: the creation is refused, and E2E creation uses a synthetic client whose attested creation is the marker (Story 5.4 AC 2).\n-- E14 (unreadable attempt history at admission), false: repeats B9; an unreadable record stops for intervention, and only a proven latest attempt qualifies.\n-- E15 (notification delivery failure), low: repeats E14/B9.\n-- E17 (live reset overrunning with no takeover path), false: Attempt ownership lets an Administrator record or an Administrator/deputy in-place recovery entry take over any attempt; only expiry itself must not abort.\n-- E19 (overlapping preparation rows), false: repeats fourth-pass E19.\n-- E20 (v2 transition), low: repeats fourth-pass E20.\n-\n ## Implementation Notes\n \n - 2026-10-30: Added canonical policy `hexalith.platform.staging-evidence-policy.v1`, README discovery and Epic 5 adoption handoff. Records the approved five rules, exact input/decision bindings, boundary examples, ownership/revisit conditions and downstream qualification obligations.\n@@ -214,42 +169,10 @@ Rejected (fifth pass):\n \n - 2026-10-30 fourth-pass review follow-up: Applied all 19 open patches. Fixed the pre-adoption v1 completion boundary and exact Platform-revision adoption binding; defined completed/superseded closure, same-release re-intake and repeated production admission under the production lock and new epoch; included preparation branches and selecting Administrator approvals in automatic triggers; refused out-of-state manual reruns with recorded reasons and assigned request authorization to Story 5.6. Completed Tenants lifecycle removal/remap review, creation/deletion classification and pre-enrollment/changed-declaration review wording; allowed one identity to approve in every recorded role and one exact record for identical predecessors. Added automatic reset issuance, the Maintainer issue mention, compatibility-precondition reference, normative record/retention requirements and Stories 6.3/6.4 qualification prerequisites. Updated README, epic context and acceptance/refusal examples. Frozen intent and all existing deferrals remain unchanged; spec/sprint final synchronization stays with the parent workflow.\n \n-- 2026-10-30 fifth-pass review follow-up: Applied all 15 open patches, using the adopted (a) options for re-intake and the checked Administrator approval. Re-intake of a release with a pending decision opens no decision and queues no attempt by itself, and only completed or superseded releases return under a new decision. The branch-determining content of the selecting approval (selected path and, for retained data, named-recovery identity, acceptance checks and maximum duration) is the checked input, so a repeating per-attempt record changes nothing. Both the preparation branch and that content join the evidence bindings and the Story 5.6 row. Executor loss now interrupts a reset and leaves it non-terminal, so lifetime notification still applies, until re-entry records it superseded; the no-replacement-job and same-point re-entry rules stay. Retention is limited to open or pending references. The trusted gate records completed closure when the latest-working pointer names the release, before that baseline change queues triggers (Story 5.6 row), and Stories 6.16, 6.15 and 6.14 are recorded as prerequisites in the Story 5.6, 5.10 and 5.11 rows and the epic context. Superseded decisions and attempts are defined at first use, and issuance order selects the latest attempt without relabelling earlier outcomes. Remaining plain imperatives became MUST. The deputy joins the bold reset-expiry recipients. The expand-only sentence restates AD-15 for staging retirements. Tenants joins the decision 4 owner column. The removal/remap example explains agreeing recorded and declared digests. The publication workflow is \"the architecture's\". The Story 5.11 row qualifies stable McpCli publication refusal. The epic-context deadline wording says \"checked at promotion admission\" and is rewrapped. README, sprint status and the deferred ledger needed no change; no new deferral was added.\n-\n ## Spec Change Log\n \n ## Review Triage Log\n \n-### Fifth-pass corrections build review, 2026-10-30\n-\n-All three independent layers completed before triage over the Story 5.1 deliverables diff from baseline `2cbb8a68` (policy, epic context and the README staging-evidence paragraph; the Story 5.2 vulnerability paragraph excluded; 41.686 kB, Blind Hunter floor seven). Verification Gap Reviewer found no gaps. Edge Case Hunter returned five findings (EC1–EC5) and Blind Hunter fourteen (BH1–BH14). Each claim was checked against the policy, the architecture's Staging gate, Staging reset, In-place recovery, Attempt ownership, Attempt resumption, Interruption outcome, Recovery-kind lifetime and Binding classes and records rows, and epics.md Stories 6.3, 6.4, 6.14, 6.15 and 6.16 before grouping. Line references are to `docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md` unless stated.\n-\n-| Finding | Verdict | Evidence and route |\n-| --- | --- | --- |\n-| EC1: Attempt queued for a decision that closes before it starts | medium | L123-126 stop only new automatic triggers once a decision is no longer pending; nothing cancels an attempt already queued for it. Candidate A's checked-input attempt queued behind B's first attempt survives B's start (A superseded, L237-240), or a queued attempt survives completed closure (L145-147), and then runs for a closed decision, writing state that may need a reset. Group G1, patch. |\n-| EC5: Rerun cases ambiguous for queued attempts of closed decisions (AC 2 claim) | medium | Same root cause as EC1: AC 2 needs unambiguous acceptance/refusal, and the policy never says whether such an attempt runs or counts as a later candidate's start. Group G1, patch. |\n-| BH1: Queued attempts outlive their decision; \"later candidate\" ordering | medium | Same root cause as EC1. Once a closed decision's queued attempt never starts, the remaining start-order question reduces to a reserved candidate's rerun, already deferred (fourth pass, Story 5.10). Group G1, patch. |\n-| EC2: Re-intake of the release production's latest-working pointer already names | medium | L154-155 let a completed release return under a new decision, and intake opens one with an automatic attempt (L97-99, L130-132). Completed closure fires on the pointer naming the release (L145-147), an event that has already happened, so the new decision stays pending, receives every trigger and its staging start supersedes real pending candidates (L237-240). Group G2, patch. |\n-| BH4: A decision for the release production already runs never completes | medium | Same root cause as EC2. Group G2, patch. |\n-| EC3: Failed reset or failed verification reissued automatically | medium | L227-228 issue \"the required reset\" automatically while a later or same-candidate attempt is blocked; L284-285 keep that attempt blocked after a failed restore or failed verification, and only executor loss and observation gaps have a defined same-point re-entry (L263-271). Read literally, the reset is reissued as a new initial entry with a fresh 3,600-second deadline (L244-245), bypassing the inheritance rule (L273-276) and the architecture's In-place recovery row (\"a failed, unverified or interrupted data restore re-enters as a data restore from the same point\"). Group G3, patch. |\n-| BH2: No next step after a failed reset or failed verification | medium | Same root cause as EC3. The observation-gap sentence already routes an unverified reset to Administrator or deputy re-entry; failed verification is also unverified, and the architecture groups failed, unverified and interrupted restores together. Group G3, patch. |\n-| EC4: Additive candidate that skipped reset leaves state for the next candidate | maybe-false | Data part false: the architecture's Staging reset row and L236-237 deliberately skip resets for proven baseline-readable additive writes, and row 3 \"exact baseline\" names the deployed release, not empty data. Unsettled part: the architecture removes environment-layer objects declared only by a reset candidate, but says nothing for an additive candidate that needed no reset; if such objects remain, a later candidate could pass staging relying on them. Settle in Story 5.10 or the architecture whether the later candidate's environment-layer attempt prunes them. Defer, medium if true, unverified. |\n-| BH3: Executor-loss rule contradicts the Interruption outcome row | false | The architecture's Records and writers rule lets only the executor, or the writer of the attempt that took it over, write an attempt record, and the takeover writer records the superseded attempt's terminal outcome as superseded. A dead executor writes nothing and the monitor writes no attempt records, so the record necessarily stays non-terminal until takeover, as L263-268 state; the Interruption outcome row's \"fails without automatic recovery\" is not a different writable terminal state. Notification and re-entry behave the same under both rows. Reject. |\n-| BH5: \"Remap\" includes pure coverage additions | low | L180-181 make any change to a flow's required associations a remap, so adding a required check to an existing flow needs owning-module and Platform approval, while adding a new flow needs none. Module developers will meet this routinely; FR-6 and frozen intent target reductions. The fix is a one-sentence clarification. Patch. |\n-| BH6: Completion timestamp refused when missing but never required | low | L89-90 and the L377 example refuse a missing or inconsistent completion timestamp, but the start-recording MUST (L76-77), the bindings (L42-53) and the Story 5.6 row (\"start/expiry recording\") never require completion time. The fix is a direct addition. The \"future\" reference-clock part repeats the rejected E1/B10 skew rationale and is not patched. Patch. |\n-| BH7: Cross-references not fixed with v1 | low | L4-5 and L336-338 fix v1 at the revision completing Story 5.1, but its rules cite PRD, epics.md anchors, architecture rows and the spec by path. A later architecture correct-course could change what an adopter reads. One sentence resolving the references at that same revision fixes it. Patch. |\n-| BH8: Epic 6 obligations never reach an Epic 6 consumer | low | Confirmed in epics.md: Story 6.4's ACs mention only the deputy, so the Platform Maintainer mention (L251-256) is owned by no story. The architecture's Attempt ownership row already makes the monitor notify on any record past its maximum lifetime, Story 6.16 needs no closure duty (Story 5.6 owns completed closure), and Stories 6.3, 6.14 and 6.15 already cover what the rows cite. `@jpiquot` currently holds both roles. Smallest fix: a Story 6.4 handoff row; epics.md alignment remains correct-course work like the existing epics.md deferral. Patch. |\n-| BH9: Older epic-context sentences contradict the policy | low | Confirmed: `epic-5-context.md` L39 keeps \"retirements remain expand-only\", L41 limits resets to incompatible writes before a later candidate and says \"Keep referenced recovery points\", and L94 limits lifecycle review to \"before gate enrollment\". Implementers of Stories 5.3–5.11 load this file as primary context. Direct sentence corrections. Patch. |\n-| BH10: Epic-context policy summary is one ~200-word sentence | low | Confirmed at `epic-5-context.md` L45-64: one semicolon-joined sentence. Implementers read it first. Split without changing content. Patch. |\n-| BH11: Multi-role clause cites nonexistent \"Maintainer approvals\" | low | L15-17 apply the multi-role rule to \"Maintainer approvals\", but decision 2 has Maintainer requests and decision 4's Platform approval names no role; L10-11 make the Platform Maintainer Platform's accountable owner. Direct wording correction naming the Platform approval by the Platform Maintainer. Patch. |\n-| BH12: Reset-expiry notification payload and record location | low | Payload part: L256-258 follow the architecture's single accepted path; its opaque evidence references carry the attempt identity, and new fields would add schema the frozen Never excludes; not patched. Location part confirmed: L252 has the monitor \"record the exceeded bound and notification\", but Records and writers let only executors write attempt records; the monitor raises issues and sets promotion-stop records. Patch to record them in the notification issue. |\n-| BH13: No stated basis for 86,400 and 3,600 seconds | low | The values are frozen user-confirmed decisions, and L6-7 already attribute them to the Platform Maintainer. Requiring measured durations adds new qualification obligations; process-only, like the earlier A13/B13 rejection. Reject. |\n-| BH14: No accepted decision-4 or first-enrollment refusal examples | low | Confirmed: both decision-4 examples (L388-389) are refusals. AC 2 covers acceptance as well as refusal for reduced-coverage cases. Two example rows (an approved removal/remap, including one record for identical predecessors, and a new lifecycle flow without Tenants review) are a direct addition. Patch. |\n-\n-Groups G1–G3 and the nine low findings are separate direct documentation patches within approved decisions 2, 3, 4 and 5; none changes frozen intent or the spec, so no loopback is required (`review_loop_iteration` stays 0). EC4 is deferred once; BH3 and BH13 are rejected.\n-\n-The original implementation agent applied G1–G3 and BH5–BH12 and BH14, in 10 policy and 2 epic-context changes. Parent inspected every corrected passage: queued-attempt cancellation, current-baseline intake, failed-reset re-entry with initial-only automatic issuance, additions exempt from removal/remap, completion-time recording, revision-resolved references, the Story 6.4 row, the Platform-approval wording, monitor recording in the issue, the two added decision-4 examples, the corrected compiled sentences and the bulleted summary. EC4 is recorded once in the deferred ledger. No unresolved patch remains.\n-\n ### Fourth-pass corrections build review, 2026-10-30\n \n All three independent layers completed before triage. Blind Hunter returned seven findings (36.852 kB; finding floor seven); Edge Case Hunter returned no findings; Verification Gap Reviewer found no verification gaps. The verdicts below precede grouping. F1–F3 are separate direct policy clarifications within the approved pending-decision, non-passing rerun and promotion-admission rules; F4 is a new unverified downstream qualification question; F5–F7 carry existing deferrals without duplication. No intent or spec loopback is required.\n@@ -318,7 +241,3 @@ Run scoped `git diff --check`; inspect new Markdown whitespace and links. Cross-\n - 2026-10-30 parent implementation audit: Independently inspected the Story 5.1 diff, all 19 fourth-pass patches, four execution tasks and four acceptance criteria. Six scoped files pass whitespace/newline checks; all 21 local Markdown links/fragments pass; frozen intent retains SHA-256 `2c4376a21f10965d0de5347fdfd277bcc30e0daaf7e6d10f3e18ce642469ac27`. No I/O matrix applies. Original baseline `2cbb8a68d277bdd55e321972b8aa11ef015fc54e` remains unchanged. A complete historical diff, including untracked files, is retained in the temporary run packet; independent subsequent work accounts for its approximately 49 MB size. Review scope isolates the Story 5.1 policy, README staging-evidence paragraph and epic handoff; historical spec claims and separately owned runtime/vulnerability changes are excluded from blind review. No tests or workloads were run for this documentation change.\n \n - 2026-10-30 fourth-pass corrections build completion: After all three review layers and the three direct corrections, parent independently repeated the scoped six-file whitespace/newline checks, 21 local link/fragment checks, all 19 original patch checks and unchanged frozen-intent verification successfully. All four execution tasks and acceptance criteria remain satisfied; no I/O matrix applies. One new unverified Story 5.11 publication-freshness question is deferred; three repeated findings retain existing deferrals. Spec is done, Story 5.1 sprint tracking moves to review, and Epic 5 remains in progress. Downstream runtime adoption and qualification remain required.\n-\n-- 2026-10-30 fifth-pass implementation verification: All 15 fifth-pass patches were cross-checked against their cited passages, consumer rows and examples, and against the architecture's Attempt ownership, Attempt resumption, Binding classes and records, Recovery point and freshness, Release modes and Empty or degraded production rows and epics Stories 5.6, 5.10, 5.11, 6.14, 6.15 and 6.16. Scoped `git diff --check` (worktree and against `HEAD`), final-newline and trailing-whitespace checks pass for the policy, README and epic context; all 21 local Markdown links and fragments resolve. The frozen-intent block is byte-identical to `HEAD`. No tests, workloads, deployments, commits, pins, submodule changes or remote publication were performed; no enforcement or owner approval is claimed. Spec and sprint final synchronization stays with the parent workflow.\n-\n-- 2026-10-30 fifth-pass corrections build completion: After all three review layers and the twelve direct corrections, parent independently repeated scoped `git diff --check`, final-newline and trailing-whitespace checks on the policy, README, epic context and spec, all 21 local link/fragment checks, table column-count consistency, and unchanged frozen-intent verification (SHA-256 `48ab3803fe19241f1c0c9c863e4c6edcc7d6ab733aa1b30c61151e37b51b79eb`, identical to the first Story 5.1 commit `92f8f051`). All 15 fifth-pass patches, four execution tasks and four acceptance criteria remain satisfied; no I/O matrix applies and no tests or workloads were run. The review diff isolates the Story 5.1 deliverables from baseline `2cbb8a68` because the complete historical diff (about 50 MB) consists almost entirely of independent work. One new unverified Story 5.10 question is deferred. Spec is done, Story 5.1 sprint tracking moves to review, and Epic 5 remains in progress."},{"sha":"5f1522d4cf0d60d362775d1591fd7210612b6d2b","filename":"docs/contracts/staging-evidence-policy-v1.md","status":"modified","additions":52,"deletions":94,"changes":146,"blob_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/blob/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/docs%2Fcontracts%2Fstaging-evidence-policy-v1.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/raw/a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699/docs%2Fcontracts%2Fstaging-evidence-policy-v1.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Hexalith/Hexalith.Platform/contents/docs%2Fcontracts%2Fstaging-evidence-policy-v1.md?ref=a57383fd34faec3a6ff891b5c63f0a9f535ed699","patch":"@@ -14,8 +14,7 @@ Platform owns the staging executor and promotion evaluator. Modules own their\n critical flows and required checks. Administrator and EventStore qualify the\n reset and recovery behavior with Platform. One identity may approve in each role\n it holds, provided the review record names every role in which it approves;\n-this applies to removal/remap approvals, including Platform approval given by the\n-Platform Maintainer, and to Tenants review.\n+this applies to removal/remap, Tenants review and Maintainer approvals.\n Publication records the approved Platform decisions; it does not record Builds\n adoption, module-owner approvals, reset qualification, runtime enforcement or\n release authorization.\n@@ -27,8 +26,6 @@ The [Story 5.1 specification](../../_bmad-output/implementation-artifacts/spec-5\n records the confirmed choices. The separate [candidate evidence and attachment\n policy v1](candidate-and-attachment-policy-v1.md) governs FR-4 candidate evidence\n and attachment holds; it does not decide staging freshness or reset semantics.\n-This policy's references to the PRD, epics, architecture and Story 5.1\n-specification resolve at the same Platform revision that fixes version 1.\n \n ## Evidence and identity boundaries\n \n@@ -49,9 +46,7 @@ insufficient: the immutable Helm package and image content identities promoted\n MUST be those qualified. Evidence MUST record declared and actually executed\n suite, runtime profile and configuration digests, the module declaration and\n flow-to-check mapping digests, the non-empty required check set and its individual completion\n-results. Evidence MUST also bind the applicable preparation branch and the\n-branch-determining content of any Administrator approval selecting it, as defined\n-in decision 2. Expected and observed identities MUST agree. The applicable production\n+results. Expected and observed identities MUST agree. The applicable production\n working baseline MUST name the release and working attempt it represents;\n absence of a baseline MUST be explicit rather than filled with an invented one.\n \n@@ -62,9 +57,7 @@ time of that check; an earlier evaluation or queued request reserves nothing.\n At that check, the evaluator MUST compare these bindings with the requested\n release, current checked inputs and current production baseline.\n Missing, unknown or mismatched bindings refuse admission, even when an outcome\n-says passed. A superseded decision is a promotion decision closed under\n-decision 5 because a later candidate's staging attempt started without an\n-Administrator reservation; it is no longer adoptable and MUST be refused,\n+says passed. A superseded decision is no longer adoptable and MUST be refused,\n even if its earlier evidence otherwise passes. Staging and production profile\n digests MUST agree, and effective infrastructure inputs MUST remain within their\n qualified sets. Evidence-validated\n@@ -78,10 +71,8 @@ prerequisites. No evidence grants publication or promotion authority by itself.\n The maximum age is **86,400 seconds (24 hours) from staging attempt start**.\n The executor MUST record the absolute UTC start when the serialized attempt\n acquires its staging lock and epoch, before preparation, deployment or candidate\n-writes. Queue time is not attempt start. The executor MUST also record the\n-attempt's absolute UTC completion time when it reaches its terminal outcome.\n-Completion, a check retry, job resumption or report publication MUST NOT restart\n-the age clock.\n+writes. Queue time is not attempt start. Completion, a check retry, job resumption\n+or report publication MUST NOT restart the age clock.\n \n The executor MUST record the immutable deadline as\n `expiresAt = attemptStartedAt + 86400 seconds`.\n@@ -100,46 +91,34 @@ point and does not abort a running restoration.\n ### 2. Attempt triggers: intake and checked-input changes\n \n Staging MUST automatically queue an attempt when a retained candidate passes\n-intake validation and opens a new pending decision under decision 3, or when an\n-input checked by the staging gate changes for a pending candidate. Re-intake of a\n-release whose decision is still pending opens no decision and queues no attempt by\n-itself; checked-input changes still queue attempts under that decision. Checked\n-inputs include required suites, critical-flow declarations and mappings, runtime\n-profile, configuration, the applicable production working baseline and\n-preparation branch, including the branch-determining content of the\n-Administrator approval selecting that branch: the selected path and, for the\n-retained-data path, the named recovery's identity, acceptance checks and maximum\n-duration. A new per-attempt Administrator record that repeats that content is no\n-checked-input change. A newly established baseline is also a baseline\n+intake validation, or when an input checked by the staging gate changes for a\n+pending candidate. Checked inputs include required suites, critical-flow\n+declarations and mappings, runtime profile, configuration, the applicable\n+production working baseline and preparation branch, including the Administrator\n+approval selecting that branch. A newly established baseline is also a baseline\n change. A changed input invalidates earlier evidence for the new input set and\n requires a fresh complete attempt, even if module versions are unchanged.\n A different retained release requires a new decision under decision 3.\n \n For a currently pending promotion decision, the Platform Maintainer may explicitly\n request a fresh attempt after evidence expires or the latest E2E attempt reaches\n a non-passing terminal outcome: failed, cancelled, incomplete, interrupted or\n-superseded. A superseded attempt is one taken over by another attempt whose writer\n-recorded the earlier attempt's terminal outcome as superseded (architecture\n-Attempt resumption); it is non-passing and may leave its decision pending. The\n-request MUST record the reason. Maintainer requests for completed or superseded decisions,\n+superseded. A superseded attempt may leave its decision pending. The request MUST\n+record the reason. Maintainer requests for completed or superseded decisions,\n or in any other state, including a fresh pass or a queued or running latest\n attempt, MUST be refused with the reason recorded. Version 1 has **no periodic retry loop**:\n expiry or a non-passing outcome alone does not authorize recurring reruns.\n Automatic triggers apply only while the candidate's decision remains\n pending; a superseded decision receives no automatic triggers, including baseline\n changes. Automatic trigger requests and manual rerun requests enter the same\n serialized staging queue and cannot bypass an outstanding reset or active lock.\n-An attempt whose decision is no longer pending when it reaches the staging lock\n-MUST be cancelled with the reason recorded; it never starts and supersedes nothing.\n \n ### 3. Rerun acceptance: latest issued attempt, complete pass\n \n When a retained candidate passes intake, the trusted gate MUST open a pending\n promotion decision bound to that exact retained release and policy unless that\n-release already has a pending one. At most one decision per retained release may be pending\n-at a time; re-intake while one is pending opens no new decision and queues no\n-attempt by itself. Intake of the release that production's latest-working pointer\n-currently names opens no decision and queues no attempt. Checked-input\n+release already has one. At most one decision per retained release may be pending\n+at a time; re-intake while one is pending opens no new decision. Checked-input\n and baseline changes queue fresh attempts under that decision. Only a different\n retained release is a changed target; switching to the Administrator-approved\n retained-data or empty/degraded path keeps the same decision. Each new staging\n@@ -150,23 +129,19 @@ a new decision identity and fresh evidence, even when its bytes match an earlier\n decision.\n \n A decision closes only as completed, when its release becomes production's\n-working baseline, or as superseded. The trusted gate MUST record completed closure\n-when production's latest-working pointer names the decision's release, before that\n-baseline change queues any automatic trigger. A non-working production release outcome leaves\n+working baseline, or as superseded. A non-working production release outcome leaves\n the decision pending, and every production release attempt for that decision MUST\n repeat promotion admission. Production recovery attempts follow the architecture's\n separate recovery rules.\n Version 1 defines no other closure. At admission, the evaluator MUST verify the\n decision's current authoritative state is pending. Completed or superseded\n decisions MUST NOT reopen under their old identity; a reopened or new decision\n-requires its own identity and fresh full evidence. A release whose decision was\n-completed or superseded may return only under a new decision with fresh evidence,\n-and only when the latest-working pointer does not name it.\n+requires its own identity and fresh full evidence. A superseded release may\n+return only under a new decision with fresh evidence.\n \n Only the **latest issued serialized staging evidence attempt** for that pending\n-decision may qualify. Issuance order, rather than completion time, selects the\n-latest attempt; that selection does not change any earlier attempt's recorded\n-outcome or label it superseded. A newer queued, running, failed, cancelled,\n+decision may qualify. Issuance order, rather than completion time, determines\n+which attempt supersedes the others. A newer queued, running, failed, cancelled,\n incomplete, interrupted or superseded attempt prevents fallback to an earlier pass. A\n qualifying rerun MUST rerun the full required suite for every included module and\n pass every required check under the same current bindings and applicable\n@@ -188,10 +163,8 @@ E2E evidence attempt, and neither is a passing E2E rerun.\n Removal means omitting a previously declared critical flow or making its required\n coverage optional. Remap means changing a flow's identity or its required\n flow-to-check associations, including a rename, split or check replacement.\n-If unchanged coverage cannot be established, the transition MUST be treated as a\n-removal or remap requiring review. Adding a new flow, or adding a required check\n-to a flow while keeping all its existing required associations, is neither a\n-removal nor a remap; Tenants review of every lifecycle flow still applies.\n+If unchanged coverage cannot be established, treat the transition as a removal\n+or remap requiring review.\n \n Before a candidate declaration containing a removal or remap can join the gate,\n the owning module and Platform MUST approve a review record binding the before\n@@ -232,37 +205,33 @@ a substitute for production smoke.\n When an unadopted candidate wrote state that production's working baseline cannot\n read, staging MUST complete a separate in-place data-restore attempt before a\n later candidate's staging attempt starts. Without a production working baseline,\n-or when its readability of the candidate's writes is unproven, those writes MUST\n-be treated as unreadable and require the same reset. A same-candidate rerun after breaking\n+or when its readability of the candidate's writes is unproven, treat those writes\n+as unreadable and require the same reset. A same-candidate rerun after breaking\n or unproven-readability writes MUST also wait for a verified reset first.\n-Staging MUST issue the required initial reset automatically ahead of the blocked\n-later-candidate or same-candidate attempt; automatic issuance covers only that\n-initial reset.\n+Staging MUST issue the required reset automatically ahead of the blocked\n+later-candidate or same-candidate attempt.\n \n The restore uses the earliest recovery point cut for that candidate since the\n last verified reset, before any of that candidate's writes, rather than a later\n rerun's point that already contains its writes. That point and its retained\n-artifacts MUST be kept while the unadopted candidate can still require a reset,\n-or while an open recovery, a pending reset or a pending named recovery references\n-them, regardless of evidence expiry. Closed lineage references in attempt history\n-do not by themselves require retention. Additive unadopted candidates whose\n-writes are prov